Behavior details:

```js
async #refresh() {
  if (!this.#supported) { this.#showDetails(); return }   // browser can't do push
  const permission = Notification.permission  // may be "default"
  const subscription = await this.#subscription
  if (subscription) {
    this.#turnOn()
  } else {
    this.#turnOff()
    if (permission === "denied") this.#showDetails()
  }
}
```

CSS: `.notifications--on` toggles `.notifications__on-message` / `.notifications__off-message` visibility. The details help blocks should be shown only when notifications are blocked (permission denied or unsupported). The design partials give them `data-notifications-target="details"` and classes `notifications-help`, plus `hide-in-browser`/`hide-in-pwa` in some cases. So the controller hides all details targets by default and unhides when blocked.

Implementation: 
```js
#showDetails() { this.detailsTargets.forEach(t => t.hidden = false) }
#hideDetails() { this.detailsTargets.forEach(t => t.hidden = true) }
```
But hidden attribute vs CSS `display: revert`? `.notifications-help` has no `hidden` handling conflict; `[hidden] { display: none !important; }` is in utilities. Good.

Careful: `hide-in-browser` / `hide-in-pwa` use media queries for display-mode. Combined with `[hidden]`, fine.

Now `toggle` action:
```js
async toggle() {
  if (await this.#subscription) {
    await this.#unsubscribe()
  } else {
    await this.#subscribe()
  }
  this.#refresh()
}
```

`#subscribe`:
```js
async #subscribe() {
  const permission = await Notification.requestPermission()
  if (permission !== "granted") { this.#showDetails(); return }

  const registration = await navigator.serviceWorker.ready
  let subscription = await registration.pushManager.getSubscription()
  if (!subscription) {
    subscription = await registration.pushManager.subscribe({
      userVisibleOnly: true,
      applicationServerKey: urlBase64ToUint8Array(this.applicationServerKeyValue)
    })
  }
  await this.#save(subscription)
}
```

`#save`:
```js
async #save(subscription) {
  const { endpoint, keys: { p256dh, auth } } = subscription.toJSON()
  const request = new FetchRequest("post", this.urlValue, {
    body: { push_subscription: { endpoint, p256dh_key: p256dh, auth_key: auth } },
    responseKind: "json"
  })
  await request.perform()
}
```
Note: `subscription.toJSON()` returns `{ endpoint, expirationTime, keys: { p256dh, auth } }` — in Chrome/Safari yes.

Hmm, Safari? Safari's PushSubscription.toJSON() also returns keys. Good.

`#unsubscribe`:
```js
async #unsubscribe() {
  const subscription = await this.#subscription
  if (!subscription) return
  await subscription.unsubscribe()
}
```

